## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## whole_improve_loop
##
## Whole-codebase improvement loop, UNIT-CONVERGENT + INCREMENTAL-COMMIT
## (ADR-055). The workspace is split by package/folder into chunks
## ("units"); the loop processes ONE unit at a time — alternating
## Claude / GPT review with a deterministic per-unit "two consecutive
## clean reviews" stability gate — and COMMITS each unit as soon as IT
## converges, BEFORE moving the cursor to the next unit. When a verdict
## is negative, apply fix-all in a fresh same-family session and
## RE-REVIEW the SAME unit (the cursor does not advance until the unit is
## clean+stable or a per-unit pass bound is hit).
##
## This replaces the old "0 commits until a GLOBAL streak of num_chunks+1
## clean sweeps converges" design (ADR-055 context): on a large repo that
## global streak was unreachable, so a long run banked ZERO of its work in
## a gitignored worktree. Now every converged unit lands its own commit on
## the run's worktree branch, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has
## already banked every unit it finished. The asymptote / streak guarantee
## (ADR-052, docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md) is PRESERVED but
## re-scoped from the whole repo to the unit, where "converge and stop" is
## actually reachable.
##
## Improvement axis: defaults to the full production-ready grid
## (security, robustness, data, concurrency, observability,
## performance, boundaries). Pass --var improvement_prompt="..."
## to override and focus the review/fix loop on a single axis
## (e.g. observability, security, perf).
##
##   Loop 1: claude_code + opus 4.7
##   Loop 2: claw + gpt-5.5
##   Loop 3: like loop 1, alternating
##
## Per-unit convergence (the asymptote, scoped to the unit):
##   - A unit is CLEAN when the current reviewer approves it with zero
##     blockers (and, in explore mode, actually opened its files).
##   - A unit CONVERGES after 2 CONSECUTIVE clean reviews of THAT unit —
##     count-based, so it is family-agnostic (in DUAL the two passes are
##     cross-family because the family flips each pass; in MONO they are the
##     single family twice — the same accepted trade-off the old count-based
##     stop made, unchanged by ADR-052 mono/dual).
##   - A converged unit is then GATED BY A PER-UNIT BUILD/TEST VERIFY
##     (ADR-055 2a): unit_verify_build + unit_verify_run run the SAME
##     deterministic, stack-agnostic build+test gate the finalization uses
##     (mirror of verify_build/verify_run) BEFORE commit_unit lands it, so
##     EVERY incremental commit is build+test green on its own — an
##     interrupted run leaves a chain of individually-green commits. A red
##     per-unit verify does NOT commit: it loops back to unit_verify_build
##     (bounded unit_verify_loop(3)) to make the unit green first; if it still
##     can't build, the unit is SKIPPED without committing (force_skip
##     semantics — its changes stay uncommitted for the next pass / the final
##     whole-tree gate / the operator), never landing broken code.
##   - A unit that will not converge is force-skipped after a bounded
##     number of passes (unit_passes) so one stubborn unit can never block
##     the whole backlog — "bounded per-unit iterations" (ADR-055).
##
## Shared reviewer↔fixer context (ADR-055 2c): the fixer records a concise
## `change_rationale` (what it changed + WHY, per unit); it is threaded
## FORWARD to the next (cross-family) reviewer of the SAME unit
## (prior_change_rationale) so that reviewer verifies the fix with the fixer's
## intent visible instead of re-deriving it — killing re-litigation at the
## source. It AUGMENTS prior_pushback / previous_scanned_areas, does not
## replace them. The reverse direction (reviewer verdict → fixer) is already
## wired via blockers + fix_plan + `session: inherit`.
##
## The RUN terminates when the ranked unit backlog has each been processed
## once (units_done >= num_chunks → stop → build/test gate → finalize),
## OR max review_loop iterations are reached (per-run bound; the persisted
## cursor / streak / units_done let a re-dispatch resume mid-backlog).
##
## False-positive resistance:
##   1. Reviewers self-rate their `confidence` (low/medium/high) and
##      are explicitly told to drop stylistic preferences.
##   2. The fixer can `pushback` on items it judges non-bloquant,
##      with a justification.
##   3. The next reviewer receives the prior pushback list and is
##      instructed to NOT re-flag those items unless evidence has
##      changed — this prevents a single false positive from
##      blocking the loop forever.
##   4. `streak_check` treats a low-confidence rejection as a
##      soft-approval (alternation continues, no fix is triggered).
##      Only medium/high-confidence rejections route to the fixer.
##
## Demonstrates four DSL features delivered together:
##   1. `round_robin` router for backend alternation
##   2. `compute` node for deterministic AND of current + previous
##      verdict (no LLM call, no shell-out)
##   3. `loop.<name>.previous_output` namespace eliminating manual
##      `previous_verdict` relay through every loop-bearing edge
##   4. Boolean expressions in `when` clauses (quoted form)
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## ─── Review topology (mono/dual) — see branch-improve-loop ─────
  ## dual = alternate claude ↔ gpt (robust, two families); mono = one
  ## family (mono_family), ~half the LLM calls; auto = resolved at
  ## LAUNCH (dual if two provider families detected, else mono).
  ## Injected by pkg/reviewtopology (CLI --review-mode / studio /
  ## dispatcher) when left "auto"; without that resolution "auto"
  ## behaves as dual (a pure non-regression). NB: this bot's per-unit
  ## convergence gate is COUNT-based (2 consecutive clean reviews of the
  ## SAME unit), already family-agnostic, so no mono OR-in is needed —
  ## mono just reviews each unit with the single family and converges on
  ## two consecutive same-family clean passes. mono_family is filled by
  ## the resolver. (ADR-052 topology preserved; ADR-055 re-scopes the
  ## count from the whole repo to the unit.)
  review_mode: string = "auto"
  mono_family: string = ""

  scope_notes: string = ""
  ## Optional override of the improvement axis. Empty (default) means
  ## "audit against the full production-ready grid defined in the
  ## review_system prompt below". When non-empty, the value REPLACES
  ## the default grid as the reviewer's and fixer's focus — only
  ## blockers that fall under this axis are reported / fixed, other
  ## findings are demoted to informational notes in scanned_areas.
  ## Example: --var improvement_prompt="Focus exclusively on
  ## observability: structured logs, traces, metrics, lost error
  ## signals."
  improvement_prompt: string = ""
  ## Optional path-scope filter (focused runs). A comma- or space-
  ## separated list of fnmatch globs matched against workspace-relative
  ## paths; empty (default) = the WHOLE workspace. A bare directory, or a
  ## `dir/**` / `dir/*` form, also matches everything under that directory.
  ## This PRUNES the chunk plan in snapshot_chunk BEFORE chunking, so a
  ## focused run (e.g. --var scope_globs=pkg/runtime) reviews only that
  ## subtree instead of paying the whole-repo sweep cost — num_chunks and
  ## the streak threshold scale to the focused set, so a focused run
  ## converges in-bound like a small repo. improvement_prompt/scope_notes
  ## are the WHAT (the axis); scope_globs is the WHERE (the files). It does
  ## NOT loosen review rigor — it only restricts which files are chunked.
  ## See the focused-run finding in docs/bot-runs/whole-improve-loop.md
  ## (2026-06-14). Example: --var scope_globs="pkg/runtime,pkg/store"
  scope_globs: string = ""
  ## Context-budget chunking (issue #12). The reviewer never sees the
  ## whole workspace at once: the deterministic `snapshot_chunk` tool
  ## groups source files by package/folder into chunks of at most this
  ## many estimated tokens (~4 bytes/token), and each review pass audits
  ## ONE chunk. This keeps a ~150k+ LoC workspace from exhausting the
  ## reviewer's context window on the first iteration. This is the HARD
  ## CEILING on chunk size; the adaptive sizing below may lower the
  ## effective budget further but never raise it above this. Default
  ## 16000 (was 30000) fits the SMALLEST default reviewer — openai/gpt-5.5
  ## over the ChatGPT forfait, whose effective window is well below the
  ## API's — with head-room for the system prompt + the cross-pass
  ## scanned_areas/pushback feedback + the model's reasoning (a 30000
  ## chunk overflowed gpt-5.5 forfait at review_loop 11, run 019ef550).
  ## Raise it for a claude-only run (huge window → fewer chunks, faster
  ## convergence); lower it on a tiny-context model.
  ## See docs/adr/011-whole-improve-loop-context-chunking.md.
  max_review_chunk_tokens: int = 16000
  ## Model-adaptive chunk sizing (B). When > 0, snapshot_chunk caps the
  ## chunk budget at reviewer_context_percent% of this value (and never
  ## above max_review_chunk_tokens), so the chunk scales to the SMALLEST
  ## reviewer's REAL context window instead of a fixed guess. Set it to
  ## that model's usable input window in tokens — e.g.
  ## --var reviewer_context_tokens=32000 for a constrained forfait model.
  ## 0 (default) = disabled: max_review_chunk_tokens is used as-is. Kept a
  ## var (not auto-detected) so the bot stays a pure DSL+tool workflow and
  ## the operator can pin the window for whatever reviewer they configure.
  reviewer_context_tokens: int = 0
  ## Percent of reviewer_context_tokens a chunk may occupy when adaptive
  ## sizing is on; the rest is reserved for the system prompt, the
  ## accumulated scanned_areas/pushback fed back each pass, and the
  ## model's reasoning/output. 45 leaves > half the window for those.
  reviewer_context_percent: int = 45
  ## Reviewer context delivery (ADR-045). "explore" (default): snapshot_chunk
  ## writes a chunk INDEX markdown and the reviewer reads the listed files on
  ## demand via its read tools — removes the context-window ceiling on chunk
  ## size and lets the agent process a chunk larger than any single prompt,
  ## guarded by the deterministic engagement check in streak_check. "inline":
  ## the chunk source is rendered into the prompt (the pre-0.4.0 / A+B path),
  ## bounded by max_review_chunk_tokens — the conservative fallback for small
  ## repos. See docs/adr/045-whole-improve-loop-progressive-context-disclosure.md.
  context_mode: string = "explore"
  ## Per-run FIX HEADROOM beyond processing every unit (ADR-055). The
  ## review_loop cap is NOT this value directly — snapshot_chunk emits a
  ## dynamic loop_max = 2*num_chunks + max_review_passes: 2 clean reviews
  ## per unit to converge it (2*num_chunks) PLUS this many extra passes to
  ## find+fix blockers before a unit converges. So a fix-free repo always
  ## gets enough passes to confirm every unit twice and land each commit in
  ## one run, and a repo needing ≤ max_review_passes fix passes converges
  ## in one run too. Repos whose unit count exceeds this bound still make
  ## bounded progress and amortize across re-dispatches via the persisted
  ## cursor / unit_streak / units_done (.whole_improve_loop.state). The
  ## default 15 keeps a single run well inside max_cost_usd / max_duration
  ## with the premium default models; raise it for more in-run fix
  ## headroom. Wired as the review_loop cap via the runtime's
  ## templated-loop-bound support (resolves to an int).
  max_review_passes: int = 15

  ## ─── MR/PR finalization (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────
  ## When open_mr=true, after the run converges, verifies and commits, the
  ## finalize_mr node pushes the run's HEAD to the forge and opens a
  ## merge/pull request. Off by default so a plain local run just leaves
  ## the commit on the operator's branch. The comment→MR trigger sets this
  ## true. This bot runs in a per-run git worktree — no `worktree:` field is
  ## declared and the engine default resolves that to "auto" — so
  ## commit_changes advances the worktree HEAD and finalize_mr pushes that
  ## branch; in cloud the worktree is carved from a fresh clone. Requires a
  ## forge_token secret (or host-authenticated gh/glab) and a push remote.
  open_mr: bool = false
  ## Branch name to push + open the MR from. Empty = derive a stable
  ## name from the run (iterion/improve/<run-id>). A --var override lets
  ## the caller pin a predictable branch.
  mr_branch: string = ""
  ## Integration base the MR targets. Empty = the repo's default branch.
  mr_base: string = ""
  ## Optional back-link target: the source issue this run answers, as
  ## "native:<id>" (local board) or a forge issue URL. When set,
  ## finalize_mr posts the opened MR's URL back as a comment on it.
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + MR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted
  ## as a read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read
  ## into a prompt. optional:true so manual/local runs that rely on
  ## host-authenticated gh/glab still work (finalize_mr falls back to
  ## host auth when the file is absent). See skills/forge-mr-create.md.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

prompt review_system:
  You are an EXTREMELY demanding, methodical code reviewer in
  charge of a GLOBAL PRODUCTION-READY review — NOT a diff
  review, NOT a "what changed recently" review, but a
  complete review of the code as it runs in production.

  REVIEW SCOPE (CRUCIAL):
  1. The workspace is reviewed like an SRE taking over the
     service: the git HEAD hash has NO privilege — a bug
     introduced six months ago counts as much as one in the
     latest commit. You MUST NOT limit yourself to recent
     commits or the diff; `git log` MAY orient you but NEVER
     scopes you.
  2. CONTEXT-BUDGET CHUNKING (issue #12): a workspace this
     size does NOT fit in one context window, so the
     deterministic `snapshot_chunk` tool has already split it
     by package/folder into bounded chunks and handed you
     EXACTLY ONE chunk for THIS pass (see "THIS PASS'S CHUNK"
     in the user message). Review that chunk thoroughly.
     - The chunk's full source is provided INLINE in the user
       message (`chunk_content`). That inline content is your
       primary artifact — you do NOT need to read it from disk.
     - NEVER emit a provisional "review is still in progress"
       verdict: you have the full chunk inline, so judge it now
       and fill `approved`/`blockers`/`confidence` from it. A
       placeholder is INVALID and breaks the convergence loop.
     - You MAY use your read tools to follow a SPECIFIC
       cross-reference into another file, but you MUST NEVER
       bulk-read, `glob **/*`, `find` without a tight path, or
       enumerate the tree: that is exactly what exhausts the
       context window and is the bug this design fixes.
     - Coverage of the WHOLE workspace is cumulative across
       passes: the chunk rotates every pass and the streak
       gate (see below) refuses to terminate until every chunk
       has been reviewed clean. So judge ONLY the chunk in
       front of you; do not speculate about unseen chunks.

  IMPROVEMENT AXIS (optional override):
  The operator can pass a targeted improvement axis in the
  user prompt (`improvement_prompt`).
  - If that axis is EMPTY: apply the full production-ready
    grid below by default (historical behaviour).
  - If that axis is NON-EMPTY: focus EXCLUSIVELY on it. The
    grid below becomes secondary — you MUST only flag as
    `blockers` issues that fall under the requested axis.
    You MAY still mention other observed issues in
    `scanned_areas` as informational notes, but you MUST
    NOT count them as blockers unless the axis explicitly
    covers them.
    CRUCIAL — a non-empty axis REDEFINES what "blocking" means
    for this run. A CONCRETE, evidence-backed finding under the
    axis (for a code-quality axis: dead/unused code, real
    duplication, a clearly over-complex or confusing construct,
    a genuinely misleading name) IS a blocker: set
    `approved=false` and put the specific smallest-change fix in
    `fix_plan` so the fixer applies it THIS pass. Do NOT
    approve-and-defer axis findings to "a future quality pass" —
    deferral defeats the run's entire purpose, and the streak
    gate would then converge on UNIMPROVED code. For axis-scoped
    items the anti-false-positive carve-out below (style/naming
    "not a blocker") is SUSPENDED. The guards that REMAIN, and
    that keep the loop convergent: (a) the finding must be
    concrete — name the file/symbol and the exact change; (b)
    the fix must be the SMALLEST change that clearly improves it;
    (c) never flag pure taste, incidental similarity, or anything
    already pushed back / already addressed. A chunk whose only
    axis observations are subjective taste — or that has no
    concrete axis finding left — is `approved=true`, so once the
    real items are fixed the streak still settles to an asymptote.

  3. Cover the production-ready axes (default grid):
       - Security: auth, input validation, secret leaks,
         injection (shell/SQL/template), path traversal,
         unsafe deserialization.
       - Robustness: exhaustive error handling, uncaught
         panics, leaking goroutines, race conditions,
         deadlocks, unbounded channels, missing timeouts,
         retries without backoff.
       - Data: schema migrations, index integrity,
         transactions, idempotence, commit ordering,
         data loss on crash.
       - Concurrency: unbalanced mutexes, lock hierarchy,
         double-close, double-spawn, atomicity bugs.
       - Observability: structured logs, metrics, traces,
         lost error signals.
       - Performance: O(n^2) on hot paths, unbounded
         memory, systematic cache misses, N+1 queries.
       - Boundaries: recovery after crash/panic,
         resilience to corrupted input, behaviour under
         saturation.
  4. WITHIN THIS CHUNK — be exhaustive:
     - Apply every relevant axis above to the files in the
       chunk. The chunk is small enough that you can and
       must read it in full — no spot-checking.
     - `previous_scanned_areas` (from input) lists surfaces
       already audited in prior passes; use it to avoid
       re-litigating settled findings, NOT to skip the
       current chunk.
     - Fill `scanned_areas` with the packages/files you
       actually audited THIS pass (e.g. the chunk label and
       the specific files). Be precise and concise.
  5. APPROVAL SEMANTICS UNDER CHUNKING (read carefully):
     - Set `approved=true` when THIS chunk has no
       production-blocking issue, even though you have only
       seen one slice of the workspace. Withholding approval
       of a clean chunk on the grounds that "I haven't seen
       the rest of the repo" is WRONG and would stall the
       loop forever — full-workspace coverage is guaranteed
       NOT by you, but by the deterministic streak gate,
       which refuses to terminate until EVERY chunk has been
       reviewed clean across a full sweep, with the reviewing
       family alternating across chunks (both families take
       part in the sweep).
     - Set `approved=false` only when THIS chunk contains a
       genuine production blocker (per the anti-false-positive
       rules below). Apply the BROADENING PRINCIPLE inside the
       chunk: if the obvious surfaces are clean, probe the
       subtle ones (concurrency, recovery, corrupted input)
       before approving — but probe within the chunk, do not
       fan out across the repo.

  ADR TRACEABILITY (apply to decisions made by the fixer
  across recent iterations of this run):
  1. Identify the technical decisions visible in the diff that
     meet the ADR-worthy bar (non-obvious trade-off,
     library/pattern choice, architectural divergence). Do NOT
     request ADRs for mechanical refactors, bug fixes, or
     trivial changes.
  2. For each ADR-worthy decision you identify, check whether
     `docs/adr/` contains an ADR (new or updated) that captures
     it.
  3. If an ADR is missing for a genuinely ADR-worthy decision,
     raise a blocker with `confidence: high` ONLY if you can
     name the decision and the alternative that should have
     been documented. If you can't, lower confidence or treat
     it as a non-blocking note instead.
  4. If an ADR exists but its content does not match what the
     code actually does (stale, vague, or rationalises a
     different decision), treat that as a real blocker — empty
     or façade ADRs defeat the purpose. Self-critique: would a
     future maintainer reading only this ADR understand the
     trade-off?
  5. Add ADR coverage to `scanned_areas` when you audited it
     (e.g. `docs/adr/003-foo.md vs pkg/foo accuracy`).

  ANTI-FALSE-POSITIVE RULES:
  1. You MUST mark `approved=false` only for issues that are
     truly blocking for production: bugs, regressions,
     security vulnerabilities, obvious dead code. Style
     preferences, naming, minor optimisations, missing
     documentation are NOT blockers. (EXCEPTION: when a
     non-empty improvement axis explicitly targets these —
     e.g. a code-quality axis covering naming/duplication/
     complexity/dead code — concrete axis findings ARE blockers
     per the IMPROVEMENT AXIS section above. This rule governs
     the default/no-axis grid and any OFF-axis observation.)
  2. Self-critique: for each blocker you consider, ask
     yourself "is this REALLY blocking in production, or is
     it a personal preference?" If you hesitate, it is NOT
     a blocker — at best a non-blocking watchpoint.
  3. Confidence: evaluate your own certainty.
       - "high" = you can point to the exact line and
         describe the production scenario that breaks.
       - "medium" = you see a probable issue but are not
         100% sure of the context.
       - "low" = doubt, intuition, no concrete evidence.
  4. If a prior review (from the other family) judged this
     code OK ON SPECIFIC SURFACES, you MUST be EXTRA strict
     before adding new blockers ON THOSE SAME surfaces — you
     need concrete evidence the other reviewer missed
     something. On surfaces NOT covered previously, restart
     from scratch.
  5. Items already pushed back by the fixer: you MUST NOT
     re-flag them unless you have new evidence.

  HUMAN ESCALATION:
  When you hit an AMBIGUITY that no amount of code reading
  can resolve (e.g. "is this behaviour intentional?", "does
  the spec say X or Y?", "must I preserve backward
  compatibility?"), call the `ask_user` tool with your
  precise question. The run will pause, the question will
  show up in the operator's terminal, and you will be
  re-invoked with the answer injected into your context (a
  `[PRIOR INTERACTION]` block will be prepended to your
  user prompt).

  Use this escalation sparingly: only when you CANNOT
  decide without human information. For ordinary technical
  judgments, decide yourself and lower `confidence` if
  you're not sure.

prompt review_user_claude:
  Review ONE chunk of the workspace at {{vars.workspace_dir}}.

  Targeted improvement axis (empty = full production-ready
  grid by default):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  Scope notes:
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  Current iteration: {{loop.review_loop.iteration}}/{{loop.review_loop.max}}

  ## THIS PASS'S CHUNK (context-budget chunking)
  Chunk {{input.chunk_index}} of {{input.num_chunks}}  ·  label: {{input.chunk_label}}
  Chunked this run: {{input.chunked}}  ·  context delivery mode: {{input.context_mode}}

  Files in this chunk:
  {{input.chunk_files}}

  CONTEXT DELIVERY (ADR-045) — follow the mode above:
  - "explore" (default): the chunk source is NOT inlined. READ each file listed
    above (they are also tabulated in the index at {{input.chunk_index_path}})
    with your read tool, then judge. Read incrementally; you may skip a file
    only if it is clearly irrelevant to the axis. Do NOT bulk-read, glob, or
    enumerate the tree beyond these listed files. You MUST record in
    `files_reviewed` the EXACT paths you opened (verbatim from the list above) —
    a verdict whose files_reviewed is empty or contains a path not in the list
    is rejected by the deterministic engagement gate.
  - "inline": the full chunk source is between the markers below; review it in
    full (use read tools only to follow a specific cross-reference) and you may
    leave files_reviewed empty.
  ----- BEGIN CHUNK SOURCE (populated in inline mode only) -----
  {{input.chunk_content}}
  ----- END CHUNK SOURCE -----

  Previous reviewer's verdict (the OTHER family,
  immediately prior iteration — empty on the first
  iteration):
  {{loop.review_loop.previous_output}}

  Surfaces already audited in prior passes (all families
  combined — use this to avoid re-litigating settled
  findings, NOT to skip the current chunk):
  {{input.previous_scanned_areas}}

  Items already pushed back by the fixer (you MUST NOT
  re-flag them without new evidence):
  {{input.prior_pushback}}

  Justification for the prior pushback:
  {{input.prior_pushback_justification}}

  Rationale the OTHER family's fixer recorded for the change
  it just applied to THIS unit (empty when no fix preceded
  this review — when present, VERIFY that fix and judge
  whether a REAL issue remains; do NOT re-derive the change
  from scratch or re-litigate what it already resolved):
  {{input.prior_change_rationale}}

  Produce a structured verdict for THIS CHUNK:
    - approved: true when THIS chunk has no production
      blocker. Do NOT withhold approval merely because you
      only saw one chunk — the streak gate guarantees full
      coverage before the loop stops (see system prompt rule
      5). false only when this chunk has a real blocker.
    - family: "claude" for this reviewer
    - blockers: genuinely blocking issues IN THIS CHUNK
      (after self-critique — exclude preferences)
    - fix_plan: concrete action plan to fix the blockers
    - confidence: "low" / "medium" / "high"
    - files_reviewed: in EXPLORE mode, the exact paths from the file list
      above that you actually opened (verbatim, no hallucinated paths). Required
      and non-empty, or the deterministic engagement gate rejects the pass.
      Leave empty in inline mode.
    - scanned_areas: packages / files you actually audited
      THIS pass (concise — e.g. the chunk label plus the
      specific files, "pkg/runtime/engine.go (recovery
      dispatch)"). Relayed to the next reviewer.

prompt review_user_gpt:
  Review ONE chunk of the workspace at {{vars.workspace_dir}}.

  Targeted improvement axis (empty = full production-ready
  grid by default):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  Scope notes:
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  Current iteration: {{loop.review_loop.iteration}}/{{loop.review_loop.max}}

  ## THIS PASS'S CHUNK (context-budget chunking)
  Chunk {{input.chunk_index}} of {{input.num_chunks}}  ·  label: {{input.chunk_label}}
  Chunked this run: {{input.chunked}}  ·  context delivery mode: {{input.context_mode}}

  Files in this chunk:
  {{input.chunk_files}}

  CONTEXT DELIVERY (ADR-045) — follow the mode above:
  - "explore" (default): the chunk source is NOT inlined. READ each file listed
    above (they are also tabulated in the index at {{input.chunk_index_path}})
    with your read tool, then judge. Read incrementally; you may skip a file
    only if it is clearly irrelevant to the axis. Do NOT bulk-read, glob, or
    enumerate the tree beyond these listed files. You MUST record in
    `files_reviewed` the EXACT paths you opened (verbatim from the list above) —
    a verdict whose files_reviewed is empty or contains a path not in the list
    is rejected by the deterministic engagement gate.
  - "inline": the full chunk source is between the markers below; review it in
    full (use read tools only to follow a specific cross-reference) and you may
    leave files_reviewed empty.
  ----- BEGIN CHUNK SOURCE (populated in inline mode only) -----
  {{input.chunk_content}}
  ----- END CHUNK SOURCE -----

  Previous reviewer's verdict (the OTHER family,
  immediately prior iteration — empty on the first
  iteration):
  {{loop.review_loop.previous_output}}

  Surfaces already audited in prior passes (all families
  combined — use this to avoid re-litigating settled
  findings, NOT to skip the current chunk):
  {{input.previous_scanned_areas}}

  Items already pushed back by the fixer (you MUST NOT
  re-flag them without new evidence):
  {{input.prior_pushback}}

  Justification for the prior pushback:
  {{input.prior_pushback_justification}}

  Rationale the OTHER family's fixer recorded for the change
  it just applied to THIS unit (empty when no fix preceded
  this review — when present, VERIFY that fix and judge
  whether a REAL issue remains; do NOT re-derive the change
  from scratch or re-litigate what it already resolved):
  {{input.prior_change_rationale}}

  Produce a structured verdict for THIS CHUNK:
    - approved: true when THIS chunk has no production
      blocker. Do NOT withhold approval merely because you
      only saw one chunk — the streak gate guarantees full
      coverage before the loop stops (see system prompt rule
      5). false only when this chunk has a real blocker.
    - family: "gpt" for this reviewer
    - blockers: genuinely blocking issues IN THIS CHUNK
      (after self-critique — exclude preferences)
    - fix_plan: concrete action plan to fix the blockers
    - confidence: "low" / "medium" / "high"
    - files_reviewed: in EXPLORE mode, the exact paths from the file list
      above that you actually opened (verbatim, no hallucinated paths). Required
      and non-empty, or the deterministic engagement gate rejects the pass.
      Leave empty in inline mode.
    - scanned_areas: packages / files you actually audited
      THIS pass (concise text). Relayed to the next reviewer.

prompt fix_system:
  You are an expert developer. You fix the blocking issues
  flagged by a reviewer by applying concrete changes to the
  code.

  CONTEXT (ADR-045): you inherit the same-family reviewer's session, so the
  files it opened this pass are already in your context. If the reviewer ran in
  explore mode and a file a blocker names is not in your context (e.g. after
  compaction), just read it again by path with your read tool before editing.

  IMPROVEMENT AXIS: if the operator set a custom
  improvement axis in their user prompt, you MUST apply
  fixes ONLY for issues that fall under that axis. You
  MUST NOT refactor along other dimensions (e.g. do NOT
  touch concurrency when the axis is "observability"),
  even if you notice issues — they will be handled by
  another run targeted on that other axis.

  You MAY REFUSE an item from the blockers list if you
  judge it is not genuinely blocking for production (style
  preference, minor optimisation, missing doc, etc.). List
  those items in `pushback` with a justification in
  `pushback_justification`. The next reviewer will be
  instructed NOT to re-flag those items, preventing a
  false positive from looping the workflow forever.

  AXIS OVERRIDE — read before pushing back: when the operator
  set a non-empty improvement axis, "it isn't production-
  blocking" is NOT a valid reason to push back an item that
  falls under that axis. The axis IS the definition of blocking
  for this run, so an on-axis item (e.g. dead code, duplication,
  over-complexity, a misleading name under a code-quality axis)
  must be FIXED, not refused. Reserve `pushback` for items that
  are genuinely OFF the axis, factually wrong, pure taste, or
  already addressed — and apply the SMALLEST change that
  resolves each on-axis item.

  Use pushback sparingly: if you push back everything, the
  code will never be improved.

  FINALIZE — DO NOT EMIT A WORK-IN-PROGRESS PLACEHOLDER:
  Your structured output is the FINAL result of this fix turn, not
  a status update. NEVER return a provisional "work in progress",
  "validating and fixing", or "will continue" summary while you have
  actually edited files — that misreports your work to the loop (the
  verdict reads as "nothing applied" even though the tree changed)
  and ships unverified edits. Before you return:
    1. Finish the edits for every blocker you intend to fix (do not
       stop halfway), or push it back with a justification.
    2. VERIFY with the project's OWN build/test tooling — the same
       commands the repo already uses (its build, its test target,
       its type-check), scoped to what you touched. If a function
       signature or contract changed, find and update ALL callers,
       not only those in this chunk: a missed caller breaks the build
       in a file the chunk reviewer never sees, and the loop cannot
       catch it. Attempt verification ONCE. If the build/test cannot
       run in this environment (e.g. a pinned toolchain is missing from
       the sandbox), do NOT keep retrying it — retrying burns your step
       budget and can cut you off mid-task, which produces exactly the
       useless placeholder this section forbids. Note the limitation and
       FINALIZE NOW; a deterministic gate re-runs the build afterwards.
    3. Set applied=true only when at least one fix is actually in the
       tree, and make `summary` state the concrete changes AND the
       verification you ran — or say plainly you could not verify and
       why. A truthful "applied, build green" or "applied but tests
       not run" always beats a placeholder.

  SHARED-CONTEXT HANDOFF (ADR-055): fill `change_rationale` with a CONCISE
  what+why for the changes you applied to THIS unit — which files/symbols you
  touched and why each change is correct/sufficient. It is threaded FORWARD to
  the next (cross-family) reviewer of the same unit, which uses it to verify
  your fix with your intent visible instead of re-deriving it from scratch.
  Keep it a rationale (a few sentences), never a transcript or a full diff, so
  the reviewer's context stays lean. (The reverse direction — the reviewer's
  verdict to you — is already wired: you receive its `blockers` + `fix_plan`
  and, via `session: inherit`, its full reasoning chain.)

  HUMAN ESCALATION:
  If a blocker is genuinely ambiguous (the spec does not
  say how to resolve it, several valid approaches conflict,
  etc.), call the `ask_user` tool with your precise
  question. The run pauses, the question shows up in the
  operator's terminal, and you will be re-invoked with the
  answer in context. Do NOT use this channel for technical
  choices you can decide yourself.

  ADR DOCUMENTATION:
  1. When fixing a blocker forces a non-trivial technical
     decision — switching to a different approach than the
     existing implementation, adopting a new pattern or
     library, accepting a new trade-off, or diverging from an
     existing convention — you MUST add or update an ADR
     markdown file under `docs/adr/`.
  2. Decisions that DO warrant an ADR:
       - Replacing one library/SDK/pattern with another.
       - Restructuring an architectural boundary the existing
         code crossed.
       - Choosing one of several reasonable fix strategies when
         the trade-off is non-obvious.
  3. Decisions that DO NOT warrant an ADR:
       - Bug fixes whose rationale is obvious from the bug.
       - Naming, style, or test-coverage tweaks.
       - Reverting to behaviour the existing spec already
         described.
  4. Follow the format of existing files in `docs/adr/` (Status,
     Context, Decision, Consequences). Number the new ADR
     sequentially (highest existing number + 1, zero-padded to
     three digits).
  5. The ADR MUST capture the trade-off that motivated the fix
     — not just describe what changed. If you cannot articulate
     the alternative you rejected, the fix was probably not
     ADR-worthy.
  6. If you UPDATE an existing ADR (status flip, supersede), add
     a dated entry rather than rewriting history.

prompt fix_user:
  For the code in {{vars.workspace_dir}}, examine this
  list of blockers and apply concrete fixes.

  Targeted improvement axis (empty = full production-ready
  grid by default, fix everything that is blocking):
  {{vars.improvement_prompt}}

  CHUNK SCOPE (issue #12): these blockers were raised against
  ONE chunk of the workspace, which you (the same-family
  reviewer) just inspected in this very session — its source
  is already in your context. Apply fixes to the files the
  blockers name (a bounded set within that chunk). Do NOT
  bulk-read or sweep the rest of the repo: other chunks are
  reviewed and fixed on their own passes.

  Reported blockers:
  {{input.blockers}}

  Proposed fix plan:
  {{input.fix_plan}}

  For EACH blocker:
    - If it is a real blocker — a production issue, OR (when an
      axis is set) a concrete item that falls under that axis:
      read the relevant file, apply the smallest correction,
      verify the tests pass.
    - If you judge it is NOT actionable — off-axis, factually
      wrong, pure taste, or already addressed: add the exact
      item to `pushback` with a clear justification in
      `pushback_justification`. Under a set axis, "not
      production-blocking" alone is NOT a valid pushback reason
      for an on-axis item.

  Output:
    - applied: true if at least one fix was applied
    - summary: summary of applied fixes
    - change_rationale: a CONCISE what+why for the changes you made to THIS
      unit — which files/symbols you touched and why each change is
      correct/sufficient (your INTENT, not a diff). The next reviewer of this
      same unit reads it to verify your fix instead of re-deriving it from
      scratch, so be specific but brief (a few sentences). Empty only if you
      applied nothing.
    - pushback: refused items (empty if all fixed)
    - pushback_justification: rationale for the pushback

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

schema review_input:
  prior_pushback: string[]
  prior_pushback_justification: string
  ## prior_change_rationale (ADR-055 2c — SHARED REVIEWER↔FIXER CONTEXT): the
  ## CONCISE what+why the OTHER family's fixer recorded for the change it just
  ## applied to THIS unit (fix_output.change_rationale, relayed on the
  ## alt -> reviewer_* edge). Read it to VERIFY the fix and judge whether a
  ## REAL issue remains, rather than re-deriving the change from scratch — the
  ## shared context that kills re-litigation at the source. Empty when no fix
  ## preceded this review (first pass on a unit). AUGMENTS prior_pushback /
  ## previous_scanned_areas; it is intent, not a diff.
  prior_change_rationale: string
  ## previous_scanned_areas accumulates the audit surfaces from
  ## ALL prior reviewer iterations (both families). The current
  ## reviewer reads it to prefer uncovered surfaces — see
  ## review_system rule 4. Empty on the first iteration.
  previous_scanned_areas: string[]
  ## Chunk fields (issue #12) — populated by the snapshot_chunk tool
  ## and carried on the alt -> reviewer edge. chunk_content is the
  ## bounded, pre-fetched source of THIS pass's chunk (≤
  ## max_review_chunk_tokens); chunk_files is the newline-separated
  ## path list; chunk_index/num_chunks/chunked describe where this
  ## chunk sits in the rotation so the reviewer knows it is seeing a
  ## slice, not the whole repo.
  chunk_content: string
  chunk_files: string
  ## ADR-045 explore mode: chunk_file_list is the array form of chunk_files
  ## (exact paths to read + cite in files_reviewed); chunk_index_path points
  ## at the index markdown; context_mode is "explore" | "inline".
  chunk_file_list: string[]
  chunk_index_path: string
  context_mode: string
  chunk_label: string
  chunk_index: int
  num_chunks: int
  chunked: bool

## snapshot_output is the deterministic workspace-snapshot tool's
## contract. It is the shared chunking surface for the reviewer (reads
## chunk_content) and, transitively, the fixer (inherits the reviewer's
## session, which already holds the chunk). The per-unit tuple (cursor +
## unit_streak + unit_passes + units_done) is persisted in a JSON state
## file (.whole_improve_loop.state at the workspace root) so re-dispatched
## runs resume mid-backlog and keep banking converged units instead of
## re-scanning the first chunks. num_chunks drives run termination
## (units_done >= num_chunks); persisted_unit_streak / persisted_unit_passes
## / persisted_units_done are the on-disk bases streak_check increments this
## pass; total_* are telemetry surfaced in the reviewer prompt.
schema snapshot_output:
  chunk_content: string
  files: string
  ## chunk_file_list (ADR-045): the chunk's files as an ARRAY of
  ## workspace-relative paths. Drives the explore-mode engagement guard in
  ## streak_check (files_reviewed must be a non-empty subset of this) and is
  ## handed to the reviewer alongside the index path.
  chunk_file_list: string[]
  ## Absolute path to the per-pass chunk INDEX markdown the reviewer reads
  ## in explore mode (empty when no files / inline mode still writes it).
  chunk_index_path: string
  chunk_label: string
  chunk_index: int
  num_chunks: int
  chunked: bool
  file_count: int
  chunk_tokens: int
  total_files: int
  total_tokens: int
  skipped_oversize: int
  loop_max: int
  ## ADR-055 per-unit convergence base, read from .whole_improve_loop.state
  ## (seeded from the prior pass's verdict via the loop-return edge, or from
  ## disk on the first pass of a re-dispatched run). streak_check increments
  ## / resets each this pass.
  ## - persisted_unit_streak: consecutive clean reviews of the CURRENT unit.
  ## - persisted_unit_passes: passes spent on the current unit (skip bound).
  ## - persisted_units_done: units processed (converged or force-skipped);
  ##   drives run termination (units_done >= num_chunks -> stop).
  persisted_unit_streak: int
  persisted_unit_passes: int
  persisted_units_done: int
  ## Rotation cursor this pass USED (chunk_index == cursor % num_chunks).
  ## Carried to streak_check, which echoes it back as carry_cursor —
  ## advancing ONLY when the current unit converges or is force-skipped —
  ## so the rotation advance is COUPLED to the verdict and persisted
  ## atomically with the per-unit counters (crash-safe; ADR-011/ADR-055).
  cursor: int

schema verdict_output:
  approved: bool
  ## family is enum-constrained: a model that hallucinates a third
  ## value (we observed gpt-5.5 emit "missing-patch" once) would
  ## otherwise bypass both family-aware fix routes and the workflow
  ## would fall through to its terminal `done` edge. The enum forces
  ## structured-output validation to reject the verdict, triggering
  ## iterion's parse_fallback retry path until the model commits to
  ## a valid family.
  family: string [enum: "claude", "gpt"]
  blockers: string[]
  fix_plan: string
  confidence: string [enum: "low", "medium", "high"]
  ## files_reviewed (ADR-045): in explore mode, the exact index paths the
  ## reviewer actually opened this pass. The deterministic engagement guard
  ## in streak_check requires this to be a non-empty SUBSET of the chunk's
  ## file list before a pass can count toward convergence — a reviewer may
  ## not approve a chunk it read nothing from, nor cite hallucinated paths.
  ## Empty/ignored in inline mode (the source is in chunk_content).
  files_reviewed: string[]
  ## scanned_areas captures the surfaces this reviewer actually
  ## audited this iteration (e.g. "pkg/runtime", "claw-code-go
  ## recovery dispatch", "concurrency around pkg/store/.lock").
  ## The accumulator passes the union to the next reviewer so
  ## subsequent iterations explore uncovered ground rather than
  ## re-walking the same files.
  scanned_areas: string[]

## streak_check relays approved/blockers/fix_plan/family from its
## input (the current reviewer's verdict) and computes a
## deterministic stop signal based on the previous reviewer's
## verdict. The `family` passthrough lets the fix routing pick the
## same-family fixer so the fix session can inherit the reviewer's
## context (and benefit from prompt-cache hits).
##
## Soft-approval: a low-confidence rejection (`approved=false` AND
## `confidence=="low"`) is treated as approved for streak/fix
## routing purposes. The reviewer's own uncertainty isn't a strong
## enough signal to trigger a fix cycle and risk a false-positive
## loop.
##
## cumulative_scanned_areas accumulates the audit surfaces from
## ALL prior iterations (both families). The accumulator pattern
## relies on `outputs.streak_check` resolving to the PREVIOUS
## iteration's output during the current expression evaluation
## (rs.outputs is updated AFTER execCompute completes), so the
## expression `unique(concat(outputs.streak_check.cumulative_scanned_areas,
## input.scanned_areas))` produces a union, tail-capped (last 40) so a reviewer's input stays bounded (avoids context_length_exceeded on long runs).
## On the first iteration `outputs.streak_check` is nil → concat
## treats it as empty → result == unique(input.scanned_areas).
schema streak_state:
  ## stop: the RUN is done — the last unit just converged (or was
  ## force-skipped) AND the whole ranked backlog has now been processed
  ## once (units_done + 1 >= num_chunks). Routes to the build/test gate +
  ## finalize (ADR-055).
  stop: bool
  ## engaged (ADR-045): did the reviewer actually open files from the chunk
  ## index this pass (explore mode)? Always true in inline mode. Surfaced for
  ## observability; the same predicate is inlined into the clean/converge
  ## predicates below (expr keys cannot reference sibling keys this pass).
  engaged: bool
  ## unit_clean: THIS pass reviewed the current unit clean (approved, zero
  ## blockers, and — in explore mode — actually opened its files).
  unit_clean: bool
  ## unit_converged: the current unit reached 2 consecutive clean reviews
  ## (unit_streak+1 >= 2) — count-based, family-agnostic (see the head
  ## comment). Routes to commit_unit, advances the cursor, and increments
  ## units_done. The per-unit analogue of the old "two consecutive
  ## cross-family approvals" (ADR-055 re-scopes it from the whole repo).
  unit_converged: bool
  ## force_skip: the current unit has churned unit_passes+1 >= the per-unit
  ## bound without converging, so we give up on it and advance the cursor
  ## anyway — "bounded per-unit iterations" (ADR-055). Guarantees one
  ## stubborn unit can never block the whole backlog. Its uncommitted
  ## partial fixes ride into the next unit's commit / the final commit.
  force_skip: bool
  approved: bool
  family: string
  blockers: string[]
  fix_plan: string
  cumulative_scanned_areas: string[]
  ## unit_streak (observability): consecutive clean reviews of the current
  ## unit THIS pass (persisted_unit_streak + 1 on a clean pass, else 0).
  unit_streak: int
  ## num_chunks is echoed from the snapshot so the stop expression can test
  ## backlog exhaustion (units_done + 1 >= num_chunks).
  num_chunks: int
  ## carry_* are the values handed BACK to snapshot_chunk on EVERY
  ## loop-return edge, persisted together as the {cursor, unit_streak,
  ## unit_passes, units_done} tuple so it is always self-consistent and
  ## crash-safe. All advance/reset iff the unit converged OR was
  ## force-skipped (ADVANCE); otherwise the loop stays on the same unit.
  ##   carry_cursor      = ADVANCE ? cursor+1        : cursor
  ##   carry_unit_streak = ADVANCE ? 0 : (clean ? persisted_unit_streak+1 : 0)
  ##   carry_unit_passes = ADVANCE ? 0               : persisted_unit_passes+1
  ##   carry_units_done  = persisted_units_done + (ADVANCE ? 1 : 0)
  carry_cursor: int
  carry_unit_streak: int
  carry_unit_passes: int
  carry_units_done: int

schema fix_input:
  blockers: string[]
  fix_plan: string

schema fix_output:
  applied: bool
  summary: string
  ## change_rationale (ADR-055 2c — SHARED REVIEWER↔FIXER CONTEXT): a CONCISE
  ## what+why for the fixes applied to THIS unit — the files/symbols touched
  ## and why each change is correct/sufficient. It is threaded FORWARD to the
  ## next (cross-family) reviewer of the SAME unit (alt -> reviewer_* edges,
  ## `prior_change_rationale`) so that reviewer verifies "is this fix good + is
  ## a real issue left?" with the fixer's intent visible, instead of
  ## re-deriving the change from scratch — killing re-litigation at the source.
  ## AUGMENTS prior_pushback / previous_scanned_areas, does not replace them.
  ## Keep it a rationale (a few sentences), never a transcript / full diff, so
  ## the reviewer's context does not bloat.
  change_rationale: string
  pushback: string[]
  pushback_justification: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## snapshot_chunk (issue #12) — the deterministic workspace-snapshot /
## chunking node, shared by the reviewer (which reads the emitted
## chunk_content) and, via session inheritance, the fixer. It groups
## source files by package/folder (Go-package boundary == directory)
## into chunks of at most {{vars.max_review_chunk_tokens}} estimated
## tokens, then emits EXACTLY ONE chunk for this pass. A package larger
## than the budget is split by file-size budget (the "package boundary
## first, then file-size" heuristic). The unit (chunk) the cursor points
## at is selected via a persisted JSON state file (.whole_improve_loop.state
## at the workspace root, add it to .gitignore) which ALSO carries the
## per-unit tuple (unit_streak + unit_passes + units_done), so a
## re-dispatched run resumes ON the current unit and keeps banking
## converged units rather than re-scanning the first chunks; deleting that
## file restarts the backlog from unit 0. No LLM — pure enumeration, so the
## reviewer never burns context discovering files (the docs-refresh
## deterministic-prepass lesson, applied to source instead of docs).
## sh -c is dash on some hosts / bash in devbox — the body is plain
## POSIX + python3 (the same toolchain sec-audit-source's tool nodes
## rely on); keep it double-quote-free so `python3 -c "..."` stays intact.
tool snapshot_chunk:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} BUDGET={{vars.max_review_chunk_tokens}} RCT={{vars.reviewer_context_tokens}} RCP={{vars.reviewer_context_percent}} MODE={{vars.context_mode}} MAXPASSES={{vars.max_review_passes}} SCOPE_GLOBS={{vars.scope_globs}} STATE_IN={{input.incoming_unit_streak}} STATE_CURSOR={{input.incoming_cursor}} STATE_PASSES={{input.incoming_unit_passes}} STATE_UNITS={{input.incoming_units_done}} python3 -c "
import os, sys, json, fnmatch

ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
# context_mode (ADR-045): 'explore' (default) emits a chunk INDEX the reviewer
# reads on demand; 'inline' renders the chunk source into chunk_content (the
# pre-0.4.0 / A+B path). Anything but 'inline' is treated as explore.
mode = (os.environ.get('MODE') or 'explore').strip().lower()
budget = int(os.environ.get('BUDGET', '16000') or '16000')
if budget <= 0:
    budget = 16000
# Model-adaptive chunk sizing (B): when reviewer_context_tokens (RCT) > 0,
# cap the chunk budget at RCP percent of the SMALLEST reviewer's real context
# window, so the inline chunk + system prompt + accumulated feedback + the
# model's reasoning never overflow that window (a 30000 chunk overflowed
# gpt-5.5 forfait at run 019ef550). Never raises the budget above the
# max_review_chunk_tokens ceiling, only lowers it.
rct = int(os.environ.get('RCT', '0') or '0')
rcp = int(os.environ.get('RCP', '45') or '45')
if rct > 0 and rcp > 0:
    adaptive = (rct * rcp) // 100
    if adaptive > 0 and adaptive < budget:
        budget = adaptive
# Hard upper bound on the per-chunk budget after A (lowered default) + B
# (adaptive). The MAX_CHUNKS rebudget below must NOT exceed this, or a large
# repo would re-inflate the chunk past the reviewer's window.
chunk_ceiling = budget

# Per-run FIX HEADROOM beyond processing every unit (ADR-055). The
# review_loop bound is emitted below as loop_max = 2*num_chunks + max_passes:
# 2 clean reviews per unit to converge it (2*num_chunks) PLUS max_passes
# extra passes to find+fix blockers before a unit converges. So a fix-free
# repo always has room to confirm every unit twice and land each commit in
# one run, and a repo needing up to max_passes fix passes converges in one
# run too; the persisted per-unit state (cursor/unit_streak/unit_passes/
# units_done) still amortizes larger backlogs across re-dispatches, each of
# which banks the units it finished.
max_passes = int(os.environ.get('MAXPASSES', '15') or '15')
if max_passes < 2:
    max_passes = 2

# Optional path-scope filter (focused runs). SCOPE_GLOBS is a comma/space-
# separated list of fnmatch patterns matched against workspace-relative
# paths; empty (default) = the whole workspace. A bare directory, or a
# 'dir/**' / 'dir/*' form, also matches everything under that directory, so
# --var scope_globs=pkg/runtime PRUNES the chunk plan to that package
# instead of paying the whole-repo sweep cost (the focused-run finding in
# docs/bot-runs/whole-improve-loop.md, 2026-06-14). resolveCommandTemplate
# shell-escapes the value as a single token (so '*' and spaces survive the
# shell without globbing/word-splitting); split it back here. Pruning at the
# os.walk source keeps total_files / total_tokens / num_chunks / the streak
# threshold all scaled to the focused set, so a focused run converges
# in-bound like a small repo.
scope_raw = os.environ.get('SCOPE_GLOBS', '') or ''
scope_globs = [g.strip() for g in scope_raw.replace(',', ' ').split() if g.strip()]


def in_scope(rel):
    if not scope_globs:
        return True
    for g in scope_globs:
        if fnmatch.fnmatch(rel, g):
            return True
        pref = g
        for suf in ('/**', '/*', '/'):
            if pref.endswith(suf):
                pref = pref[:-len(suf)]
                break
        if pref and (rel == pref or rel.startswith(pref + '/')):
            return True
    return False

# Persistent state (ADR-055): ONE json dotfile at the workspace root holds
# the per-unit tuple {cursor, unit_streak, unit_passes, units_done}. Why
# persisted: the review_loop counter and streak_check's output are run-local
# and reset on every (re-)dispatch, so without on-disk state a backlog whose
# num_chunks exceeds one run's loop_max could never be finished across ANY
# number of runs. The tuple accumulates across runs here, so each re-dispatch
# resumes mid-backlog and banks the units it converges (units_done advances
# toward num_chunks) — what makes 'the run terminates when the backlog is
# processed once' reachable on a large repo. Neutral, gitignore-able.
# Best-effort: a read-only FS degrades to cursor 0 / streak 0 (still bounded,
# just non-advancing). See docs/adr/055-unit-convergent-adaptive-improve-loop.md.
state_path = os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.state')
cursor = 0
disk_streak = 0
disk_passes = 0
disk_units = 0
try:
    with open(state_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as sf:
        st = json.load(sf)
    # Valid JSON that is not an object (a bare list/scalar/null, e.g. a
    # hand-edit or a foreign writer of this gitignore-able dotfile) has no
    # .get and would raise AttributeError -- which the except tuple below
    # does NOT catch, crashing snapshot_chunk (the workflow entry + every
    # loop-return target) and bricking the whole loop. Coerce to {} so the
    # per-unit state falls through to its 0 defaults: the same best-effort
    # degrade this block already gives a missing/garbage/non-int state file.
    if not isinstance(st, dict):
        st = {}
    cursor = int(st.get('cursor', 0) or 0)
    # ADR-055 per-unit state. unit_streak = consecutive clean reviews of
    # the CURRENT unit; unit_passes = passes spent on the current unit
    # (force-skip bound); units_done = units processed (converged OR
    # force-skipped) so far -- drives run termination (units_done >=
    # num_chunks). 'clean_streak' fallback reads a pre-ADR-055 state file.
    disk_streak = int(st.get('unit_streak', st.get('clean_streak', 0)) or 0)
    disk_passes = int(st.get('unit_passes', 0) or 0)
    disk_units = int(st.get('units_done', 0) or 0)
except (OSError, IOError, ValueError, TypeError):
    cursor = 0
    disk_streak = 0
    disk_passes = 0
    disk_units = 0
if cursor < 0:
    cursor = 0
if disk_streak < 0:
    disk_streak = 0
if disk_passes < 0:
    disk_passes = 0
if disk_units < 0:
    disk_units = 0

# State threading for THIS pass (issue #12, crash-safe). snapshot_chunk
# is the ONLY filesystem writer in the loop and it runs BEFORE the
# reviewer, so it can only ever persist verdicts up to the PREVIOUS pass.
# The cursor AND the clean_streak are therefore both fed back from
# streak_check on the loop-return edge (STATE_CURSOR / STATE_IN) and
# persisted together (see the persist below), so the on-disk pair
# {cursor, clean_streak} is always self-consistent: clean_streak counts
# the consecutive clean passes for the chunks STRICTLY BEFORE cursor, and
# 'cursor' names the chunk this pass is about to (re-)review.
#
# Crash-safety (the bug this replaces): the cursor is NOT advanced
# eagerly here. It advances only via streak_check's next_cursor, AFTER a
# verdict exists. So if the run dies on a non-clean pass before the next
# snapshot persists streak_check's reset (the common case: the fix_* node
# failing on budget/rate-limit/cancel, which routes to 'fail' + a
# re-dispatch), disk still points cursor AT the un-verdicted chunk, and
# the re-dispatch RE-REVIEWS it instead of skipping past it. The previous
# design advanced cursor+1 eagerly while the streak lagged one pass, so a
# crash could leave cursor PAST a blocker chunk with a stale-high streak,
# and the shortened terminating window converged ('stop' -> done) without
# the blocker chunk ever being re-reviewed -- a silent false convergence.
# See docs/adr/011-whole-improve-loop-context-chunking.md -> Corrections
# (2026-06-02c).
#
# On the FIRST pass of a (re-)dispatched run there is no inbound edge:
# STATE_IN / STATE_CURSOR render as an empty/literal placeholder, the
# float() parse fails, and we seed BOTH from disk instead. float() (not
# int()) tolerates a value the expr engine stringifies as e.g. '6' or
# '6.0'.
clean_streak = disk_streak
try:
    incoming = int(float((os.environ.get('STATE_IN', '') or '').strip()))
    if incoming >= 0:
        clean_streak = incoming
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    pass
# Cursor base for THIS pass: the edge-fed carry_cursor (authoritative,
# verdict-coupled) when present, else the disk cursor on a fresh dispatch.
try:
    incoming_cursor = int(float((os.environ.get('STATE_CURSOR', '') or '').strip()))
    if incoming_cursor >= 0:
        cursor = incoming_cursor
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    pass
# Per-unit pass counter + units-done counter, threaded the same way
# (edge-fed carry value wins, else disk on a fresh dispatch).
unit_passes = disk_passes
try:
    incoming_passes = int(float((os.environ.get('STATE_PASSES', '') or '').strip()))
    if incoming_passes >= 0:
        unit_passes = incoming_passes
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    pass
units_done = disk_units
try:
    incoming_units = int(float((os.environ.get('STATE_UNITS', '') or '').strip()))
    if incoming_units >= 0:
        units_done = incoming_units
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    pass

EXCLUDED_DIRS = {
    'vendor', 'node_modules', 'env', 'dist', 'build', 'out', 'target',
    '__pycache__', 'testdata', 'coverage',
}
SOURCE_EXTS = {
    '.go', '.py', '.js', '.mjs', '.cjs', '.jsx', '.ts', '.tsx', '.java',
    '.rb', '.rs', '.c', '.h', '.cc', '.cpp', '.hpp', '.cxx', '.hxx',
    '.cs', '.php', '.swift', '.kt', '.kts', '.scala', '.sh', '.bash',
    '.zsh', '.sql', '.proto', '.tf', '.gradle', '.groovy', '.lua', '.ex',
    '.exs', '.erl', '.clj', '.dart', '.vue', '.svelte', '.ml', '.mli',
    '.hs', '.jl', '.r', '.pl', '.pm',
}
SKIP_SUFFIXES = ('.min.js', '.min.css', '.pb.go', '_pb2.py', '.gen.go',
                 '.generated.go', '.g.dart')
MAX_FILES = 20000
MAX_FILE_TOKENS = budget


def est_tokens(nbytes):
    return (nbytes + 3) // 4


pkgs = {}
total_files = 0
total_tokens = 0
truncated_scan = False
for root, dirs, names in os.walk(ws):
    # Prune dependency/build dirs AND every hidden dir (.git, .devbox,
    # .iterion, .works, .github, .archive, .claude, .venv, ...).
    dirs[:] = sorted(d for d in dirs if d not in EXCLUDED_DIRS and not d.startswith('.'))
    for n in sorted(names):
        ext = os.path.splitext(n)[1].lower()
        if ext not in SOURCE_EXTS:
            continue
        if any(n.endswith(s) for s in SKIP_SUFFIXES):
            continue
        full = os.path.join(root, n)
        try:
            if os.path.islink(full):
                continue
            sz = os.path.getsize(full)
        except OSError:
            continue
        rel = os.path.relpath(full, ws)
        if not in_scope(rel):
            continue
        tok = est_tokens(sz)
        pkgs.setdefault(os.path.dirname(rel) or '.', []).append((rel, tok))
        total_files += 1
        total_tokens += tok
        if total_files >= MAX_FILES:
            truncated_scan = True
            break
    if truncated_scan:
        break


def pack(pkgs, budget):
    chunks = []
    cur = {'files': [], 'tokens': 0, 'dirs': []}

    def flush():
        nonlocal cur
        if cur['files']:
            chunks.append(cur)
            cur = {'files': [], 'tokens': 0, 'dirs': []}

    for d in sorted(pkgs.keys()):
        files = pkgs[d]
        pkg_tokens = sum(t for _, t in files)
        if pkg_tokens <= budget:
            if cur['tokens'] + pkg_tokens > budget and cur['files']:
                flush()
            cur['files'].extend(files)
            cur['tokens'] += pkg_tokens
            cur['dirs'].append(d)
        else:
            flush()
            sub = {'files': [], 'tokens': 0, 'dirs': [d]}
            for rel, tok in files:
                if sub['tokens'] + tok > budget and sub['files']:
                    chunks.append(sub)
                    sub = {'files': [], 'tokens': 0, 'dirs': [d]}
                sub['files'].append((rel, tok))
                sub['tokens'] += tok
            if sub['files']:
                chunks.append(sub)
    flush()
    return chunks


chunks = pack(pkgs, budget)
num_chunks = len(chunks)

# ADR-055: each unit needs 2 clean reviews to converge, so the loop bound is
# loop_max = 2*num_chunks + max_passes (emitted below). Cap the unit COUNT on
# huge repos by re-packing with a larger budget, so that bound stays within a
# sane pass count instead of 200+ passes / fragile multi-run amortization.
MAX_CHUNKS = 24
if num_chunks > MAX_CHUNKS and total_tokens > 0:
    # Respect the adaptive/ceiling budget: never raise the chunk size above
    # chunk_ceiling just to cap the chunk COUNT, or a large repo on a small
    # reviewer window would overflow again. If the ceiling forces > MAX_CHUNKS
    # chunks, accept the higher pass count (loop_max scales with num_chunks).
    budget = min(chunk_ceiling, (total_tokens // MAX_CHUNKS) + 1)
    chunks = pack(pkgs, budget)
    num_chunks = len(chunks)

if num_chunks == 0:
    try:
        with open(state_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as sf:
            json.dump({'cursor': 0, 'unit_streak': int(clean_streak),
                       'unit_passes': int(unit_passes),
                       'units_done': int(units_done), 'num_chunks': 1}, sf)
    except (OSError, IOError):
        pass
    print(json.dumps({
        'chunk_content': '(no source files found in workspace)',
        'files': '', 'chunk_file_list': [], 'chunk_index_path': '',
        'chunk_label': 'empty', 'chunk_index': 0,
        'num_chunks': 1, 'loop_max': 2 + max_passes, 'chunked': False, 'file_count': 0,
        'chunk_tokens': 0, 'total_files': 0, 'total_tokens': 0,
        'skipped_oversize': 0, 'persisted_unit_streak': int(clean_streak),
        'persisted_unit_passes': int(unit_passes),
        'persisted_units_done': int(units_done),
        'cursor': int(cursor),
    }))
    sys.exit(0)

idx = cursor % num_chunks
chunk = chunks[idx]
# Persist the (cursor, clean_streak) pair this pass is USING -- NOT an
# eager cursor+1. The advance to the next chunk rides streak_check's
# next_cursor on the loop-return edge, so the cursor only moves once a
# verdict has been folded in. This keeps disk crash-safe: a run that dies
# mid-pass leaves cursor pointing at the chunk under review, so a
# re-dispatch re-reviews it rather than skipping past it (issue #12,
# ADR-011 -> Corrections 2026-06-02c).
try:
    with open(state_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as sf:
        json.dump({'cursor': cursor, 'unit_streak': int(clean_streak),
                   'unit_passes': int(unit_passes),
                   'units_done': int(units_done),
                   'num_chunks': num_chunks}, sf)
except (OSError, IOError):
    pass

file_list_arr = [rel for rel, _ in chunk['files']]
file_list = '\n'.join(file_list_arr)
dirs = chunk['dirs']
if len(dirs) == 1:
    label = dirs[0]
else:
    label = '%s (+%d more dirs)' % (dirs[0], len(dirs) - 1)

# ADR-045: always write a per-pass chunk INDEX markdown (label + the file
# table the reviewer reads from in explore mode). Single file overwritten each
# pass, excluded from commit_changes. Best-effort: a write failure is non-fatal
# (the reviewer still has chunk_file_list / files).
index_path = os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.chunk.md')
try:
    ilines = ['# Review chunk index', '',
              'Chunk %d of %d  -  %s' % (idx, num_chunks, label),
              'Read each file below with your read tool, then judge. List the',
              'paths you read VERBATIM in files_reviewed.', '',
              '| file | ~tokens |', '| --- | --- |']
    for rel, tok in chunk['files']:
        ilines.append('| %s | %d |' % (rel, tok))
    ilines.append('')
    with open(index_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as xf:
        xf.write('\n'.join(ilines))
except (OSError, IOError):
    pass

skipped_oversize = 0
if mode == 'inline':
    hard_cap = budget * 4 + 4096
    parts = []
    used = 0
    for rel, tok in chunk['files']:
        abs_path = os.path.join(ws, rel)
        try:
            with open(abs_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as f:
                body = f.read()
        except (OSError, IOError):
            continue
        if est_tokens(len(body)) > MAX_FILE_TOKENS:
            body = body[:MAX_FILE_TOKENS * 4] + '\n... [truncated: file exceeds one chunk budget] ...\n'
            skipped_oversize += 1
        header = '===== FILE: %s (~%d tokens) =====\n' % (rel, tok)
        block = header + body + '\n'
        if used + len(block) > hard_cap:
            parts.append('... [chunk content truncated at the char cap] ...\n')
            break
        parts.append(block)
        used += len(block)
    content = ''.join(parts)
else:
    content = ('(explore mode: chunk source is NOT inlined. Read the files in '
               'the index at %s -- also in chunk_file_list -- with your read '
               'tool, then judge.)' % index_path)

print(json.dumps({
    'chunk_content': content,
    'files': file_list,
    'chunk_file_list': file_list_arr,
    'chunk_index_path': index_path,
    'chunk_label': label,
    'chunk_index': idx,
    'num_chunks': num_chunks,
    'chunked': num_chunks > 1,
    'file_count': len(chunk['files']),
    'chunk_tokens': chunk['tokens'],
    'total_files': total_files,
    'total_tokens': total_tokens,
    'skipped_oversize': skipped_oversize,
    # ADR-055 per-unit state, carried to streak_check which increments /
    # resets each and echoes the carry_* values back on the loop-return
    # edge so the persisted {cursor, unit_streak, unit_passes, units_done}
    # tuple stays verdict-coupled and crash-safe.
    'persisted_unit_streak': int(clean_streak),
    'persisted_unit_passes': int(unit_passes),
    'persisted_units_done': int(units_done),
    # Rotation cursor this pass USED (idx == cursor % num_chunks). Unlike
    # the pre-ADR-055 design it advances ONLY when the current unit
    # converges or is force-skipped (streak_check's carry_cursor), so the
    # loop stays on a unit until it is clean+stable -- the per-unit
    # convergence scoping.
    'cursor': int(cursor),
    # 2 clean reviews per unit to converge (2*num_chunks) + fix headroom.
    'loop_max': 2 * num_chunks + max_passes,
}))
"`
  output: snapshot_output

## Family selector (see branch-improve-loop for the full rationale):
## round_robin → condition so a MONO run routes to a single family
## (round_robin ignores `when` guards). The GPT edge is guarded; the
## CLAUDE edge is the unconditional default (C012). Parity rides
## loop.review_loop.iteration (this router is not a review_loop entry,
## so loop-backs never reset it — lockstep with the old counter).
router alt:
  mode: condition

judge reviewer_claude:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-max}"
  input: review_input
  output: verdict_output
  ## Persist the verdict as a versioned artifact so each iteration's
  ## approval / blockers / fix_plan is inspectable in the studio's
  ## Artifact tab (the VerdictCard renders it) and diffable across the
  ## review loop. Without publish:, the verdict lives only in
  ## events.jsonl / run.log and never reaches the Artifact tab.
  publish: verdict_claude
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_claude
  session: fresh
  ## When the LLM hits genuine uncertainty (ambiguous spec, missing
  ## context, impossible-to-decide trade-off), it can escalate to
  ## the human running the CLI by emitting `_needs_interaction:
  ## true` + `_interaction_questions: {...}` in its JSON output.
  ## The runtime catches this, pauses, prompts the dev in their
  ## terminal, and re-invokes the reviewer with the answer mapped
  ## into its input — preserving session continuity.
  interaction: human

judge reviewer_gpt:
  ## Explicit backend pin: model "openai/gpt-5.5" only works via the
  ## claw backend. Without this, the auto-resolver falls back to
  ## ITERION_BACKEND_PREFERENCE (default "claude_code,claw") and
  ## routes the node through claude_code, which doesn't recognise
  ## openai/* models — the agent returns a meta-error string instead
  ## of the structured verdict and the schema validator fails the run.
  backend: "claw"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-5.5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"
  input: review_input
  output: verdict_output
  ## Persist the verdict (see reviewer_claude's publish: note).
  publish: verdict_gpt
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_gpt
  session: fresh
  ## claw backend uses snake_case tool names. Without these the
  ## reviewer can't read the workspace and produces a
  ## "no filesystem access" verdict — the claw runtime exposes
  ## these via tool.RegisterClawBuiltins.
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  interaction: human

## Deterministic PER-UNIT convergence detector (ADR-055; supersedes the
## coverage-aware global streak of ADR-011 / issue #12).
##
## The loop works ONE unit (chunk) at a time. Each pass reviews the unit
## the cursor currently points at. streak_check turns the reviewer's
## verdict into per-unit control state (all predicates fully inlined
## because a compute node's keys cannot reference each other this pass):
##
##   unit_clean     — this pass approved the unit with zero blockers (and,
##                    in explore mode, actually opened its files: the
##                    ADR-045 engagement guard, so a "read nothing then
##                    approve" pass cannot count).
##   unit_streak    — CONSECUTIVE clean reviews of THIS unit; +1 on a clean
##                    pass, reset to 0 on any non-clean pass. Base read from
##                    input.persisted_unit_streak (snapshot_chunk's on-disk
##                    value), not a self-reference — the same nil-safe,
##                    crash-safe pattern ADR-011 established.
##   unit_converged — unit_clean AND unit_streak+1 >= 2. Two consecutive
##                    clean reviews of the SAME unit. COUNT-based, so it is
##                    family-agnostic: in DUAL the two passes are
##                    cross-family (the condition router flips family each
##                    pass); in MONO they are the single family twice — the
##                    same accepted trade-off the old count-based stop made,
##                    so ADR-052 mono/dual needs NO change here. A converged
##                    unit is COMMITTED (commit_unit) and the cursor advances.
##   force_skip     — NOT converged AND unit_passes+1 >= 8. A unit the loop
##                    cannot satisfy within a bounded number of passes is
##                    skipped so it can never block the whole backlog
##                    ("bounded per-unit iterations", ADR-055). The cursor
##                    advances without a commit; partial fixes ride into a
##                    later commit.
##   stop           — (unit_converged OR force_skip) AND units_done+1 >=
##                    num_chunks. The RUN ends when the whole ranked backlog
##                    has been processed once — a finite, monotone process,
##                    NOT a global clean sweep that a real repo can never
##                    satisfy (the ADR-055 failure this replaces).
##
## CRASH-SAFE, CROSS-RUN. carry_cursor / carry_unit_streak /
## carry_unit_passes / carry_units_done are the {cursor, unit_streak,
## unit_passes, units_done} tuple every loop-return edge hands back to
## snapshot_chunk, which persists it to .whole_improve_loop.state. All four
## are verdict-coupled: the cursor (and units_done) advance ONLY once a
## verdict exists (converged or skipped), so a run that dies mid-unit leaves
## the cursor AT the un-finished unit and a re-dispatch RE-REVIEWS it. Every
## unit that DID converge is already committed, so a crash/cap strands
## nothing. A large repo whose backlog exceeds one run's loop_max makes
## bounded progress and finishes over successive re-dispatches.
##
## The `approved` passthrough field stays soft — it includes low-confidence
## rejections — so the downstream `when approved` edge keeps re-reviewing
## the same unit (family flipping) without invoking the fixer when a
## reviewer is merely uncertain. Real, blocker-free approvals are what build
## the per-unit streak toward convergence.
compute streak_check:
  output: streak_state
  expr:
    ## ADR-045 engagement guard (inlined everywhere below because expr keys
    ## cannot reference sibling keys this pass). In explore mode the chunk
    ## source is NOT in the prompt, so a pass only counts as clean if the
    ## reviewer actually opened files from the index: files_reviewed must be
    ## non-empty AND a subset of chunk_file_list (the identity |A∪B| == |A|
    ## ⟺ B ⊆ A, using only concat/unique/length). In inline mode the source
    ## IS in the prompt, so the guard short-circuits true. This blocks the
    ## Goodhart failure of approving a chunk having read nothing / citing
    ## hallucinated paths.
    ##
    ## ADR-055 per-unit convergence. The predicates below (fully inlined):
    ##   CLEAN     = approved AND zero blockers AND engaged (this pass)
    ##   CONVERGED = CLEAN AND persisted_unit_streak+1 >= 2  (2 consecutive
    ##               clean reviews of THIS unit — count-based, family-agnostic)
    ##   FORCE-SKIP= NOT CONVERGED AND persisted_unit_passes+1 >= 8  (give up
    ##               on a stubborn unit so it can't block the backlog)
    ##   ADVANCE   = CONVERGED OR FORCE-SKIP  (move the cursor to the next unit)
    ## carry_* echo the {cursor, unit_streak, unit_passes, units_done} tuple
    ## back to snapshot_chunk on every loop-return edge, verdict-coupled and
    ## crash-safe (the unit only advances once a verdict exists). stop fires
    ## when the last unit advances AND the whole backlog is processed
    ## (units_done+1 >= num_chunks).
    engaged: "(input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))"
    unit_clean: "(input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list)))))"
    unit_converged: "((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2)"
    force_skip: "(!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)"
    stop: "(((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) || (!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)) && input.persisted_units_done + 1 >= input.num_chunks"
    approved: "input.approved || input.confidence == 'low'"
    family: "input.family"
    blockers: "input.blockers"
    fix_plan: "input.fix_plan"
    num_chunks: "input.num_chunks"
    unit_streak: "if((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))), input.persisted_unit_streak + 1, 0)"
    carry_cursor: "if((((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) || (!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)), input.cursor + 1, input.cursor)"
    carry_unit_streak: "if((((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) || (!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)), 0, if((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))), input.persisted_unit_streak + 1, 0))"
    carry_unit_passes: "if((((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) || (!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)), 0, input.persisted_unit_passes + 1)"
    carry_units_done: "input.persisted_units_done + if((((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) || (!((input.approved && length(input.blockers) == 0 && (input.context_mode != 'explore' || (length(input.files_reviewed) > 0 && length(unique(concat(input.chunk_file_list, input.files_reviewed))) == length(unique(input.chunk_file_list))))) && input.persisted_unit_streak + 1 >= 2) && input.persisted_unit_passes + 1 >= 8)), 1, 0)"
    cumulative_scanned_areas: "tail(unique(concat(outputs.streak_check.cumulative_scanned_areas, input.scanned_areas)), 40)"

## Per-family fixers. Each one inherits the SAME-family reviewer's
## session via `session: inherit` + `_session_id` mapping on the
## edge — so the fixer reuses the prompt cache, the workspace files
## already loaded, and the reviewer's reasoning chain. The result
## is one session per loop iteration per family (not two), which
## both shortens latency and dramatically reduces token cost:
## without inheritance every fixer would re-scan the workspace.
agent fix_claude:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-max}"
  input: fix_input
  output: fix_output
  system: fix_system
  user: fix_user
  session: inherit
  ## Same escalation contract as the reviewers — the fixer can ask
  ## the dev a question (e.g. clarify the intent of an ambiguous
  ## blocker) instead of guessing.
  interaction: human

agent fix_gpt:
  ## See reviewer_gpt comment — same backend pin rationale.
  backend: "claw"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-5.5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"
  input: fix_input
  output: fix_output
  system: fix_system
  user: fix_user
  session: inherit
  ## claw needs the same read/write toolset the reviewer used so
  ## the fixer can actually apply edits.
  tools: [bash, read_file, write_file, file_edit, glob, grep]
  ## 45 (was 30): a multi-file fix (read several files + edit + verify) can
  ## legitimately need more than 30 tool steps; getting cut off mid-edit at the
  ## cap leaves a partial edit + an empty final turn that the formatting pass
  ## renders as a useless "work in progress" placeholder (run 019ec9d5). The
  ## FINALIZE "verify once, don't loop" guard is the primary mitigation; this is
  ## the backstop headroom.
  tool_max_steps: 45
  interaction: human

## ─── Commit (close the no-commit gap) ──────────────────────────
## whole_improve_loop historically converged (streak `stop`) straight to
## `done` with NO commit step, so its validated work stayed uncommitted in
## the worktree — HEAD never advanced past base, finalizeWorktree recorded
## nothing (final_commit=None), and the diff was stranded. Commit ALL
## changes on convergence so the engine records a final_commit + GC-guard
## branch like feature_dev / docs-refresh. `git add -A` because the loop
## touches the whole workspace, not a known file list; no-op-safe — a clean
## tree skips the commit via `diff --cached --quiet`.
## ─── Build/test gate (deterministic, before commit) ───────────────
## A whole_improve_loop reviewer reads only its CHUNK'S SOURCE, so a fix
## that breaks compilation in a file OUTSIDE the current chunk (e.g. a
## renamed/retyped symbol with a caller the chunk reviewer never saw) — or
## a bug only a test catches — is invisible to the review loop. The loop
## could then rebuild a clean streak and converge on a BROKEN tree (observed
## 2026-06-14, run 019ec7ed: a fixer left a write-only ReadAt + a missed
## out-of-scope caller; both passed source review and the loop kept going).
##
## So before committing, an ADAPTIVE agent (skill-guided, stack-agnostic)
## builds + tests the repo with its OWN tooling and writes a re-runnable
## script; a DETERMINISTIC tool node re-runs that script and gates the commit
## on the REAL exit code (no LLM judgment). Red build/tests => the run does
## NOT commit: it loops back to the agent to fix the breakage (bounded), and
## fails loudly if it cannot. This is the CLAUDE.md "skill-guided + determin-
## istic gate" doctrine (cf. sec-audit-source's scan_health). UNIVERSAL: no
## language or package manager is named in this DSL — the agent reads
## skills/verify-build.md and dispatches to whatever THIS repo uses.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. The review loop just converged on a set
  of changes its reviewers approved by reading SOURCE — but each
  reviewer sees only ONE chunk at a time, so a change that breaks
  compilation in another file (a renamed/retyped symbol with a caller
  the chunk reviewer never saw) or a bug only a test catches can slip
  through. Your single job: make this repository actually BUILD and its
  tests PASS using the repo's OWN tooling, and leave a script the
  deterministic gate can re-run.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the
     `verify-build` skill. Use the repo's own toolchain: a task runner /
     Makefile target, a package-manager test script, or a language
     build+test command, HONOURING any pinned toolchain (version
     managers, lockfiles, devbox, GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to
     `{{vars.workspace_dir}}/.whole_improve_loop.verify.sh` that runs the
     repo's build AND its tests (or the closest fast equivalent) and
     exits non-zero on any failure. Keep it to the commands a developer
     of THIS repo would run; no network-heavy or destructive steps.
  3. Run it. If it FAILS, the failure is almost always introduced by the
     just-applied review fixes — read the error and FIX it at the source
     (update the missed caller, correct the signature, fix the test) with
     the SMALLEST change that restores build+tests. Do NOT refactor or
     change behaviour beyond what is needed to compile and pass. Re-run
     until green.
  4. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config),
     write a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node will RE-RUN your script and gate the commit on
  its real exit code, so claiming "it passes" without making it pass is
  pointless. Output prepared=true once
  `.whole_improve_loop.verify.sh` exists and reflects the repo's real
  build+test; summary = the command you settled on and what you fixed.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Make it build + test green with its OWN tooling and write
  `.whole_improve_loop.verify.sh` (see the system prompt + the
  `verify-build` skill).

  Output from the PREVIOUS verify attempt's deterministic gate (empty on
  the first attempt — when present, fix exactly what it shows, it is the
  real build/test failure):
  {{input.fail_log}}

schema verify_input:
  fail_log: string

schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is set from
## the real process exit code of re-running .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh —
## NOT an LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was
## produced (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  ## high, not max: this is mechanical build-repair work, not deep review.
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: verify_input
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  ## fresh: a distinct task from the chunk reviewers/fixers; it inspects the
  ## whole tree's buildability, not a single inherited chunk session.
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run is the DETERMINISTIC half: it re-runs the agent-prepared
## .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. No language
## or package manager appears here — it just executes whatever the adaptive
## verify_build agent wrote, so the gate stays universal while the verdict
## stays deterministic. Runs in the same sandbox the agent used, so the
## toolchain it found is present. python3 (the snapshot_chunk toolchain) is
## used for safe JSON + subprocess capture; keep the body double-quote-free.
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
script = os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
try:
    with open(os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## ─── PER-UNIT build/test gate (ADR-055 2a) ─────────────────────────
## Every incremental commit_unit must be BUILD+TEST GREEN ON ITS OWN, so an
## interrupted run leaves a chain of individually-green commits (not just the
## final commit gated by verify_build/verify_run). Historically commit_unit
## landed a converged unit with NO build/test gate, so an intermediate commit
## could compile-break the tree (a chunk reviewer approves from chunk SOURCE
## and never sees a cross-file caller / a test-only bug).
##
## unit_verify_build + unit_verify_run MIRROR the finalization gate
## verify_build/verify_run EXACTLY — same prompts (verify_system/verify_user),
## same schemas (verify_input/verify_plan/verify_result), same deterministic
## re-run of .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh gated on the REAL exit code. They
## are separate NODES only so the routing differs (commit_unit + loop back to
## the backlog, vs commit_changes + finalize). STACK-AGNOSTIC by construction:
## no language / package manager is named here — the adaptive agent reads
## skills/verify-build.md and dispatches to whatever THIS repo uses, identical
## to verify_build. The per-unit verify→fix sub-loop is bounded by
## unit_verify_loop(3), exactly like the final verify_loop(3).
agent unit_verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  ## high, not max: mechanical build-repair work, not deep review (see verify_build).
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: verify_input
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  ## fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a single inherited chunk session
  ## (same rationale as verify_build).
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## Deterministic half of the per-unit gate — re-runs the agent-prepared
## .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. Byte-identical
## to verify_run's body (no language/package manager named); a distinct node so
## its `when passed` edge routes to commit_unit instead of commit_changes.
tool unit_verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
script = os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'unit_verify_build produced no .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh; per-unit build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- unit_verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[unit verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'unit_verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
try:
    with open(os.path.join(ws, '.whole_improve_loop.verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

schema commit_result:
  success: bool
  output: string

schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  ## opened: true when a merge/pull request was created (or already
  ## existed for the branch). url: the MR/PR URL. branch: the pushed
  ## branch. back_linked: true when the URL was posted to source_issue_ref.
  ## skipped_reason: precise reason when opened=false (no forge auth, no
  ## remote, nothing to push) — never a silent no-op.
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string

## commit_unit — ADR-055 INCREMENTAL COMMIT. Fires the moment a UNIT (chunk)
## converges (streak_check.unit_converged), BEFORE the cursor advances to the
## next unit, so every completed unit is banked on the run's worktree branch
## and an interrupted / budget-capped run keeps all the units it finished
## (the old design committed nothing until an unreachable global streak).
## Includes UNTRACKED files the fixer created — `git add -A` stages new files
## too (the right-artifact rule: `git diff HEAD` the reviewers use omits
## untracked, so commits MUST include them, or a feature that ADDS files never
## lands). EXCLUDES the bot's own scratch dotfiles (state/verify/chunk) exactly
## like commit_changes — `add -A` then `reset` is ignore-agnostic (see the
## commit_changes note). Message carries the unit's chunk label. The env-prefix
## `LABEL=…` on the git commit shell-escapes the (possibly space/paren-bearing)
## label as one token; ${LABEL:-workspace} guards an empty label. No-op-safe:
## `diff --cached --quiet` makes no empty commit when a unit converged with no
## code change (a re-review that only confirmed cleanliness).
tool commit_unit:
  command: `git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} add -A && { git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} reset -q -- .whole_improve_loop.state .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh .whole_improve_loop.verify.log .whole_improve_loop.chunk.md >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} diff --cached --quiet || LABEL={{input.label}} git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} commit -m "chore(improve): ${LABEL:-workspace} production-readiness pass"; }`
  output: commit_result

## commit_changes is the FINALIZATION commit on `stop` (backlog processed):
## the per-unit commit_unit already landed each converged unit, so this catches
## the LAST unit (whose stop pass routes straight to the build/test gate, not
## through commit_unit) plus any fix the verify_build gate had to apply to make
## the whole tree build+test green. EXCLUDES the bot's own
## .whole_improve_loop.state rotation/streak scratch file (committing it would
## pollute the target repo's history). We stage everything then unstage the
## state file — NOT an `:!.whole_improve_loop.state` exclude-pathspec: when the
## target already gitignores the state file (iterion does, see this repo's
## .gitignore), git treats the excluded-but-ignored path as explicitly named
## and aborts `add` with "paths are ignored by .gitignore, use -f" (exit 1
## under the sandbox git), failing the whole convergence at the finish line.
## `add -A` + `reset` is ignore-agnostic: add -A naturally skips the file when
## it's ignored, reset unstages it when it's not. Bonus: when the finalization
## has nothing left to commit (all units already landed, verify made no fix),
## nothing stays staged and the `diff --cached --quiet` guard makes NO empty
## commit.
tool commit_changes:
  command: `git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} add -A && { git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} reset -q -- .whole_improve_loop.state .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh .whole_improve_loop.verify.log .whole_improve_loop.chunk.md >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} diff --cached --quiet || git -C {{vars.workspace_dir}} commit -m "chore(improve): production-readiness pass (whole_improve_loop)"; }`
  output: commit_result

## ─── Nodes : MR/PR finalization (opt-in) ────────────────────────

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output field so the two
## outgoing edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form (when open_mr /
## when not open_mr). No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a converged improvement run by opening ONE merge/pull
  request for the commit this run produced, then (optionally) posting
  the MR link back to the source issue. You make NO further code changes.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and
  open the MR/PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of
       the base (`git rev-list --count <base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do
       NOT open an empty MR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/improve/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set, else
       the repo's default branch — see the skill).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token
       file when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is
       available, set opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never
       pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the MR/PR
       from that branch into the base, with a clear title + body
       summarising the improvements (use scope_notes for context).
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened MR URL back to it: a
       forge issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>"
       ref → use the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short
  summary. Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → repo default branch): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Context / scope: {{input.scope_notes}}

  Push the branch, open the MR/PR, post the back-link when a source issue
  is given, and return the structured result.

## finalize_mr — opt-in MR/PR creation. claude_code so the bundled
## forge-mr-create skill is mirrored and the board comment tool (for a
## native back-link) is available. Gated by mr_gate (open_mr).
agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_WHOLE_IMPROVE_MR_MODEL:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_WHOLE_IMPROVE_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  tool_max_steps: 40

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow whole_improve_loop:
  ## Entry is the deterministic chunker, not the round-robin router:
  ## every pass re-snapshots the workspace (picking up the fixer's
  ## edits) and selects this pass's chunk BEFORE the reviewer runs.
  entry: snapshot_chunk

  ## Container isolation. Reviewers run `git diff`, the project's
  ## tests, linters, smoke-runs, etc. — all of which we'd rather not expose
  ## to the host. Inline mode (vs `mode: auto`) chosen so the
  ## post_create script that installs claude inside the container is
  ## honoured — see feature_dev/main.bot for the full rationale.
  sandbox:
    ## See feature_dev/main.bot for the rationale on full vs slim.
    image: "ghcr.io/socialgouv/iterion-sandbox-full:edge"
    user: "1000:1000"
    mounts: ["type=bind,source=${localEnv:HOME}/.claude,target=/home/devbox/.claude,consistency=cached"]
    env:
      CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: "/home/devbox/.claude"
    post_create: "set -e; claude --version >/dev/null 2>&1 || sudo npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code@latest; claude --version >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo 'claude install verification failed' >&2; exit 1; }"

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h"
    max_cost_usd: 60

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## snapshot_chunk (the chunker) runs first each pass, then hands off
  ## to the round-robin router. Its output (this pass's chunk) is read
  ## by both reviewer edges below.
  snapshot_chunk -> alt

  ## Round-robin alternates: 1st pass → claude, 2nd → gpt, repeat.
  ## Each reviewer receives THIS pass's chunk (from snapshot_chunk) plus
  ## the OTHER family's most recent fixer pushback so it knows which
  ## items to skip — the alternation guarantees the cross-family
  ## pushback comes from the previous iteration's fixer of the opposing
  ## family. On the first iteration both `outputs.fix_*` are empty →
  ## fields render as empty strings and the prompt's "do not re-flag
  ## these items" section is harmless.
  alt -> reviewer_claude with {
    prior_pushback: "{{outputs.fix_gpt.pushback}}",
    prior_pushback_justification: "{{outputs.fix_gpt.pushback_justification}}",
    prior_change_rationale: "{{outputs.fix_gpt.change_rationale}}",
    previous_scanned_areas: "{{outputs.streak_check.cumulative_scanned_areas}}",
    chunk_content: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_content}}",
    chunk_files: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.files}}",
    chunk_file_list: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_file_list}}",
    chunk_index_path: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_index_path}}",
    context_mode: "{{vars.context_mode}}",
    chunk_label: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_label}}",
    chunk_index: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_index}}",
    num_chunks: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.num_chunks}}",
    chunked: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunked}}"
  }
  alt -> reviewer_gpt when "if(vars.review_mode == 'mono', vars.mono_family == 'gpt', loop.review_loop.iteration % 2 == 1)" with {
    prior_pushback: "{{outputs.fix_claude.pushback}}",
    prior_pushback_justification: "{{outputs.fix_claude.pushback_justification}}",
    prior_change_rationale: "{{outputs.fix_claude.change_rationale}}",
    previous_scanned_areas: "{{outputs.streak_check.cumulative_scanned_areas}}",
    chunk_content: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_content}}",
    chunk_files: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.files}}",
    chunk_file_list: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_file_list}}",
    chunk_index_path: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_index_path}}",
    context_mode: "{{vars.context_mode}}",
    chunk_label: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_label}}",
    chunk_index: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_index}}",
    num_chunks: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.num_chunks}}",
    chunked: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunked}}"
  }

  ## Both reviewers feed into streak_check. The loop is attached
  ## here so the runtime snapshots the reviewer's verdict as
  ## `loop.review_loop.previous_output` for the NEXT iteration to
  ## inspect — eliminating the manual `previous_verdict` relay.
  reviewer_claude -> streak_check as review_loop("{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.loop_max}}") with {
    approved: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.approved}}",
    family: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.family}}",
    blockers: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.blockers}}",
    fix_plan: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.fix_plan}}",
    confidence: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.confidence}}",
    scanned_areas: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.scanned_areas}}",
    files_reviewed: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude.files_reviewed}}",
    chunk_file_list: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_file_list}}",
    context_mode: "{{vars.context_mode}}",
    num_chunks: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.num_chunks}}",
    persisted_unit_streak: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_unit_streak}}",
    persisted_unit_passes: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_unit_passes}}",
    persisted_units_done: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_units_done}}",
    cursor: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.cursor}}"
  }
  reviewer_gpt -> streak_check as review_loop("{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.loop_max}}") with {
    approved: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.approved}}",
    family: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.family}}",
    blockers: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.blockers}}",
    fix_plan: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.fix_plan}}",
    confidence: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.confidence}}",
    scanned_areas: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.scanned_areas}}",
    files_reviewed: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt.files_reviewed}}",
    chunk_file_list: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_file_list}}",
    context_mode: "{{vars.context_mode}}",
    num_chunks: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.num_chunks}}",
    persisted_unit_streak: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_unit_streak}}",
    persisted_unit_passes: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_unit_passes}}",
    persisted_units_done: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.persisted_units_done}}",
    cursor: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.cursor}}"
  }

  ## RUN termination (ADR-055): the last unit just advanced AND the whole
  ## backlog is processed (units_done+1 >= num_chunks) → run the deterministic
  ## build/test gate BEFORE the finalization commit. Per-unit commits already
  ## landed each earlier unit; this gate catches a cross-file build break or a
  ## test-only bug the chunk reviewers could not see (they approve from chunk
  ## source only — see the verify_build/verify_run section). verify_build
  ## adapts to the repo's own tooling and fixes any breakage; verify_run
  ## re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code. This is checked FIRST among
  ## streak_check's edges, so a converged last unit finalizes here rather than
  ## routing to commit_unit.
  streak_check -> verify_build when stop with { fail_log: "" }
  verify_build -> verify_run
  ## Green build+tests → finalization commit (last unit + any verify fix), THEN
  ## finish.
  verify_run -> commit_changes when passed
  ## Red → loop back to verify_build to fix the breakage, bounded. The gate's
  ## log_tail is handed back as the failure to fix.
  verify_run -> verify_build as verify_loop(3) with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.verify_run.log_tail}}"
  }
  ## Still red after the bounded retries → FAIL (never commit a broken tree;
  ## the converge-on-broken backstop). The operator inspects the worktree +
  ## the .whole_improve_loop.verify.log.
  verify_run -> fail

  ## After committing, route through mr_gate: open an MR when open_mr,
  ## else finish. finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD + opens the MR/PR
  ## and (when source_issue_ref is set) posts the URL back to the issue.
  commit_changes -> mr_gate

  mr_gate -> finalize_mr when open_mr with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}"
  }
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr

  finalize_mr -> done

  ## ADR-055 INCREMENTAL COMMIT + PER-UNIT BUILD/TEST GATE (2a). A UNIT
  ## converged (2 consecutive clean reviews) and it is NOT the last unit (else
  ## `stop` above caught it). Before banking it, run the SAME deterministic
  ## build/test gate the finalization uses — unit_verify_build (adaptive,
  ## stack-agnostic, writes .whole_improve_loop.verify.sh) + unit_verify_run
  ## (re-runs it, gates on the REAL exit code) — so every incremental commit is
  ## individually green. A chunk reviewer approves from chunk SOURCE only and
  ## can't see a cross-file compile break or a test-only bug; this gate can.
  ## Listed AFTER the stop edge so a converged LAST unit finalizes via stop,
  ## not here (first-match-wins).
  streak_check -> unit_verify_build when unit_converged with { fail_log: "" }
  unit_verify_build -> unit_verify_run
  ## GREEN per-unit build+tests → commit the unit NOW (before the cursor
  ## advances) so it is banked even if the run is later interrupted. commit_unit
  ## carries the unit's chunk label for the message. snapshot_chunk's output is
  ## still available this pass, so the label resolves here.
  unit_verify_run -> commit_unit when passed with {
    label: "{{outputs.snapshot_chunk.chunk_label}}"
  }
  ## RED → loop back to unit_verify_build to fix the breakage, bounded by
  ## unit_verify_loop(3) (mirrors the final verify_loop(3)). The gate's log_tail
  ## is handed back as the exact failure to fix, so the unit is made green
  ## BEFORE it commits.
  unit_verify_run -> unit_verify_build as unit_verify_loop(3) with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.unit_verify_run.log_tail}}"
  }
  ## Still RED after the bounded retries → SKIP the unit WITHOUT committing
  ## broken code (force_skip semantics, ADR-055 "bounded per-unit iterations"):
  ## advance the cursor via the ADVANCE carry tuple streak_check already
  ## computed for this converged unit (carry_cursor = cursor+1,
  ## carry_units_done = units_done+1), leaving the unit's changes UNCOMMITTED in
  ## the working tree for the next pass / the final whole-tree verify_build gate
  ## (which will try to fix them, then `fail` loudly if it can't) / the
  ## operator. Consistent with force_skip (advance, count the unit done, leave
  ## partial fixes uncommitted) — one un-buildable unit can never block the
  ## backlog, and NO broken commit is ever landed. Listed last (unconditional
  ## fallthrough), taken only once unit_verify_loop(3) is exhausted.
  unit_verify_run -> snapshot_chunk with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }
  ## After committing the unit, loop back to snapshot_chunk with the ADVANCED
  ## carry tuple (carry_cursor = cursor+1, carry_unit_streak = 0,
  ## carry_unit_passes = 0, carry_units_done = units_done+1) so the next pass
  ## reviews the NEXT unit. Not loop-bearing (the review_loop counter advances
  ## on the reviewer→streak_check edge); still bounded by that counter.
  commit_unit -> snapshot_chunk with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }

  ## FORCE-SKIP (ADR-055 "bounded per-unit iterations"): the current unit has
  ## churned unit_passes+1 >= the per-unit bound without converging (and it is
  ## not the last-unit stop case). Advance the cursor anyway (carry_cursor =
  ## cursor+1, carry_units_done = units_done+1) so one stubborn unit can never
  ## block the whole backlog. Its uncommitted partial fixes stay in the tree
  ## and ride into the next unit's commit / the final finalization commit —
  ## nothing is lost. Listed before the soft-approve edge so a clean-but-capped
  ## pass still advances.
  streak_check -> snapshot_chunk when force_skip with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }

  ## Otherwise approved (unit clean but not yet stable — needs a second
  ## consecutive clean review, OR low-confidence rejection treated as
  ## soft-approved, OR rejection without concrete blockers): keep reviewing
  ## the SAME unit without fixing. Routes back to snapshot_chunk (not alt);
  ## because carry_cursor DID NOT advance (unit not converged/skipped this
  ## pass) the next pass re-reviews the same chunk, and snapshot_chunk -> alt
  ## flips the family — building the per-unit streak toward convergence.
  ## Expression form is kept so future authors can tighten the soft-approval
  ## predicate.
  ##
  ## NOTE: this edge does NOT carry `review_loop` so the loop
  ## counter (and the previous_output snapshot) is incremented
  ## ONCE per cycle on the reviewer→streak_check edge above. The
  ## cycle is still bounded because that edge IS loop-bearing.
  ## It hands the carry tuple back to snapshot_chunk so the next snapshot
  ## re-persists {cursor, unit_streak, unit_passes, units_done} to
  ## .whole_improve_loop.state (crash-safe cross-run resume — ADR-055).
  streak_check -> snapshot_chunk when "approved" with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }

  ## Medium/high-confidence rejection WITH concrete blockers: route
  ## to the SAME-family fixer so it can inherit the reviewer's
  ## session (one session per iteration per family — see
  ## fix_claude / fix_gpt comments). `_session_id` reads directly
  ## from the most recent reviewer output rather than going through
  ## streak_check, because compute passthrough strips the
  ## underscore-prefixed metadata fields.
  ##
  ## The `length(blockers) > 0` guard avoids invoking the fixer
  ## when the reviewer rejected on coverage grounds alone (e.g.
  ## "cumulative coverage is partial at iteration 0/15") with no
  ## production-blocking issue identified. In that case the fixer
  ## has nothing to fix — burning a session round-trip and tokens
  ## (~$0.15/$0.43 observed in vibe_review run_1777560043656) only
  ## to push back. The unconditional alt fallthrough below picks
  ## those up so alternation continues without a wasted fix call.
  ##
  ## Edge order matters: iterion evaluates conditional edges top-
  ## down, first match wins. We list both family-specific fixers
  ## before the unconditional `done` fallthrough so loop-exhaustion
  ## still terminates cleanly.
  streak_check -> fix_claude when "!approved && family == 'claude' && length(blockers) > 0" with {
    blockers: "{{outputs.streak_check.blockers}}",
    fix_plan: "{{outputs.streak_check.fix_plan}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.reviewer_claude._session_id}}"
  }
  streak_check -> fix_gpt when "!approved && family == 'gpt' && length(blockers) > 0" with {
    blockers: "{{outputs.streak_check.blockers}}",
    fix_plan: "{{outputs.streak_check.fix_plan}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.reviewer_gpt._session_id}}"
  }

  ## Recovery cycle uses a separate loop name so its counter doesn't
  ## interfere with the review_loop snapshot semantics. Sized large
  ## enough to allow review_loop to be the binding constraint. Both
  ## fixers share the loop counter — total fix attempts across both
  ## families are capped at 20. Routes back to snapshot_chunk. ADR-055:
  ## because this pass had blockers the unit did NOT converge, so
  ## carry_cursor does NOT advance — the next pass RE-REVIEWS the SAME
  ## (just-fixed) unit rather than moving on, which is the per-unit
  ## review→fix→re-review loop. carry_unit_streak is 0 (a blocker resets it)
  ## and carry_unit_passes increments toward the force-skip bound, so a unit
  ## the fixer can never satisfy is eventually skipped instead of grinding
  ## forever. The carry tuple is persisted to disk crash-safely.
  fix_claude -> snapshot_chunk as recovery_loop(20) with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }
  fix_gpt -> snapshot_chunk as recovery_loop(20) with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }

  ## Loop exhaustion fallbacks: when a bounded loop hits its limit
  ## the loop-bearing edges above are skipped and execution falls
  ## through to these terminal edges. Route to `fail`, NOT `done`:
  ## exhausting the review/recovery loop WITHOUT streak_check ever
  ## firing `stop` means this run did not reach convergence — reporting
  ## `finished` there is a silent empty success, and the dispatcher would
  ## mark the ticket done/review as if the whole workspace were clean.
  ## Legit success runs through streak_check's `stop` edge → done.
  ##
  ## ON LARGE REPOS THIS IS EXPECTED, NOT A DEAD END (ADR-055): when the
  ## unit backlog exceeds what max_review_passes allows in one run (loop_max =
  ## 2*num_chunks + max_review_passes), a single run cannot process every unit,
  ## so it exhausts review_loop and lands here — but the per-unit tuple
  ## {cursor, unit_streak, unit_passes, units_done} is PERSISTED to
  ## .whole_improve_loop.state, so a re-dispatch (or a manual re-run of the
  ## acceptance command) resumes mid-backlog and keeps banking converged units
  ## (each already committed) until units_done reaches num_chunks. `fail` is
  ## the per-run signal "backlog not finished yet"; the operator/dispatcher
  ## re-runs until a run exits via `stop`. Crucially, unlike the old design a
  ## `fail` here still LEFT COMMITTED WORK behind (every unit that converged
  ## this run), so nothing is stranded. Tune max_review_passes up (with the
  ## budget) to finish in fewer runs. See README "Large workspaces".
  reviewer_claude -> fail
  reviewer_gpt    -> fail
  fix_claude      -> fail
  fix_gpt         -> fail
  ## streak_check's catch-all routes back to snapshot_chunk (not a
  ## terminal) so an unmatched conditional state (e.g. a hallucinated
  ## family that escapes the enum, or any future expression edge case)
  ## keeps the loop cycling rather than silently terminating. The
  ## bounded review_loop counter on the reviewer→streak_check edges
  ## still guarantees the workflow eventually exits — exhaustion routes
  ## via the reviewer→fail fallthrough above. Carries the per-unit tuple
  ## back like the other loop-return edges so the persisted state stays
  ## coherent even on this rare path (ADR-055).
  streak_check    -> snapshot_chunk with {
    incoming_unit_streak: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_streak}}",
    incoming_cursor: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_cursor}}",
    incoming_unit_passes: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_unit_passes}}",
    incoming_units_done: "{{outputs.streak_check.carry_units_done}}"
  }
